## What you may change

| Path | Owner | Rule |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `prototypes/` | You | Interactive prototypes, one self-contained HTML file per screen or flow. |
| `assets/` | You | Exported images, screenshots and illustrations the prototypes or notes refer to. |
| `notes/decisions.md` | You | Design decisions, open questions and rejected alternatives. |
| `handoff/implementation-map.md` | You and Claude Code | One row per screen: prototype files, status and acceptance notes. |
| Everything else | Generated | Do not edit. `{{syncCommand}}` regenerates it from the project model and refuses to overwrite hand edits. |

## How to design here

- Start from the screens in `context/screens.md`. Keep their IDs and titles. Propose a new screen,
  component or interaction in `notes/decisions.md` and add a `proposed` row to the implementation
  map; do not silently add structure the project model does not have.
- Use the token names in `context/design-tokens.md` for colour, spacing, radius and typography, as
  CSS custom properties with a neutral fallback, for example `var(--text-normal, #222)`. Do not
  introduce a second palette or hard-coded brand colours.
- Every screen works in the light and the dark theme, at narrow width, and from the keyboard with a
  visible focus indicator. Text has sufficient contrast in both themes.
- Name prototype files `<screen-id>--<variant>.html`, for example `node-1--compact.html`. Keep each
  file self-contained: inline CSS and JavaScript, sample data only, no remote fonts, scripts, images
  or network requests.
- Put `data-design-id="<id>"` on the element that represents a screen, component or interaction from
  `context/screens.md`, so the implementation can be mapped back without guessing.
- Show empty, loading, error and populated states for every screen that lists or edits data.
- Describe behaviour with sample content. Do not invent backend behaviour, storage or permissions.

## Hand off to Claude Code

When a prototype is ready to build:

1. Set its row in `handoff/implementation-map.md` to `ready`, list its prototype files and write the
   acceptance notes a reviewer can check.
2. Save the files into this folder and commit them, or hand the folder back to the developer.
3. The developer asks Claude Code: *"Implement the ready rows of
   `{{folder}}/handoff/implementation-map.md`, following `{{folder}}/handoff/HANDOFF.md`."*

Claude Code follows `handoff/HANDOFF.md` and the repository's own `AGENTS.md`.
